{"version":3,"kind":"Article","sha256":"394e4413f86c54bd6f518e00e2906ded9dd275e4fcc120858f68c11be94a147a","slug":"pages.m12-behavior-of-sample-proportion-1-of-3","location":"/pages/166-m12-behavior-of-sample-proportion-1-of-3.md","dependencies":[],"frontmatter":{"title":"The Sample Proportion: Center, Spread, and Shape","content_includes_title":false,"authors":[{"nameParsed":{"literal":"City College of San Francisco","given":"City College","non_dropping_particle":"of","family":"San Francisco"},"name":"City College of San Francisco","id":"contributors-myst-generated-uid-0"}],"license":{"content":{"id":"CC-BY-NC-SA-4.0","url":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-sa/4.0/","name":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial Share Alike 4.0 International","CC":true}},"numbering":{"title":{"offset":2}},"exports":[{"format":"md","filename":"166-m12-behavior-of-sample-proportion-1-of-3.md","url":"/textbook/build/166-m12-behavior-of--0908b8e8eacbbe8a65373b944896dbfc.md"}]},"mdast":{"type":"root","children":[{"type":"block","children":[{"type":"admonition","children":[{"type":"admonitionTitle","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Learning Objectives","position":{"start":{"line":3,"column":1},"end":{"line":3,"column":1}},"key":"s1FE5W9z6X"}],"key":"xksvvTBvWA"},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"spread":false,"position":{"start":{"line":6,"column":1},"end":{"line":6,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"listItem","spread":true,"position":{"start":{"line":6,"column":1},"end":{"line":6,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Apply the sampling distribution of the sample proportion (when appropriate). In particular, be able to identify unusual samples from a given population.","position":{"start":{"line":6,"column":1},"end":{"line":6,"column":1}},"key":"G33TCd6pjA"}],"key":"tEjSIMnAm9"}],"key":"okZL7KIJLk"}],"key":"HSNRzn83th"}],"class":"note","key":"J6F5RySJOc"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":9,"column":1},"end":{"line":9,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"The first step to drawing conclusions about parameters based on the accompanying statistics is to understand how sample statistics behave relative to the parameter that summarizes the entire population. We begin with the behavior of sample proportion relative to population proportion (when the variable of interest is categorical). After that, we will explore the behavior of sample mean relative to population mean (when the variable of interest is quantitative).","position":{"start":{"line":9,"column":1},"end":{"line":9,"column":1}},"key":"zJ2NjLyDx3"}],"key":"iAbUaNfyLq"},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"position":{"start":{"line":11,"column":1},"end":{"line":11,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"Behavior of Sample Proportion (p-hat)","position":{"start":{"line":11,"column":1},"end":{"line":11,"column":1}},"key":"o3JJ8Fs6CU"}],"identifier":"behavior-of-sample-proportion-p-hat","label":"Behavior of Sample Proportion (p-hat)","html_id":"behavior-of-sample-proportion-p-hat","implicit":true,"key":"G7lUi5RfI4"},{"type":"admonition","children":[{"type":"admonitionTitle","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Example: Part-Time College Students","position":{"start":{"line":13,"column":1},"end":{"line":13,"column":1}},"key":"yNhy30OfyK"}],"key":"k8rLBiM5cE"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":16,"column":1},"end":{"line":16,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"Approximately 60% of all part-time college students in the United States are female. (In other words, the population proportion of females among part-time college students is p = 0.6.) What would you expect to see in terms of the behavior of the sample proportion of females (","position":{"start":{"line":16,"column":1},"end":{"line":16,"column":1}},"key":"IH3Vv3OQtr"},{"type":"inlineMath","value":"\\hat{p}","position":{"start":{"line":16,"column":1},"end":{"line":16,"column":1}},"html":"<span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mover accent=\"true\"><mi>p</mi><mo>^</mo></mover></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\hat{p}</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.8889em;vertical-align:-0.1944em;\"></span><span class=\"mord accent\"><span class=\"vlist-t vlist-t2\"><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.6944em;\"><span style=\"top:-3em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:3em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\">p</span></span><span style=\"top:-3em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:3em;\"></span><span class=\"accent-body\" style=\"left:-0.1667em;\"><span class=\"mord\">^</span></span></span></span><span class=\"vlist-s\">​</span></span><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.1944em;\"><span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span>","key":"NyYpWXiLfE"},{"type":"text","value":") if random samples of size 100 were taken from the population of all part-time college students?","position":{"start":{"line":16,"column":1},"end":{"line":16,"column":1}},"key":"yh4WriGTJ0"}],"key":"Swqfte9TbN"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":18,"column":1},"end":{"line":18,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"As we saw before, due to sampling variability, sample proportion in random samples of size 100 will take numerical values which vary according to the laws of chance: in other words, sample proportion is a ","position":{"start":{"line":18,"column":1},"end":{"line":18,"column":1}},"key":"RKW79oXk6j"},{"type":"emphasis","position":{"start":{"line":18,"column":1},"end":{"line":18,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"random variable","position":{"start":{"line":18,"column":1},"end":{"line":18,"column":1}},"key":"fFkfRVpuIr"}],"key":"un29T5ycV3"},{"type":"text","value":". To summarize the behavior of any random variable, we focus on three features of its distribution: the center, the spread, and the shape.","position":{"start":{"line":18,"column":1},"end":{"line":18,"column":1}},"key":"OPbxOscCKO"}],"key":"kHqx6xYatF"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":20,"column":1},"end":{"line":20,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"Based only on our intuition, we would expect the following:","position":{"start":{"line":20,"column":1},"end":{"line":20,"column":1}},"key":"w6LHh5aJI2"}],"key":"RrG0eOC3Ff"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":22,"column":1},"end":{"line":22,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"emphasis","position":{"start":{"line":22,"column":1},"end":{"line":22,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"Center","position":{"start":{"line":22,"column":1},"end":{"line":22,"column":1}},"key":"JvizhiJ5dM"}],"key":"vAXr0RLclz"},{"type":"text","value":": Some sample proportions will be on the low side—say, 0.55 or 0.58—while others will be on the high side—say, 0.61 or 0.66. It is reasonable to expect all the sample proportions in repeated random samples to average out to the underlying population proportion, 0.6. In other words, the mean of the distribution of ","position":{"start":{"line":22,"column":1},"end":{"line":22,"column":1}},"key":"dtGTJHxdja"},{"type":"inlineMath","value":"\\hat{p}","position":{"start":{"line":22,"column":1},"end":{"line":22,"column":1}},"html":"<span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mover accent=\"true\"><mi>p</mi><mo>^</mo></mover></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\hat{p}</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.8889em;vertical-align:-0.1944em;\"></span><span class=\"mord accent\"><span class=\"vlist-t vlist-t2\"><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.6944em;\"><span style=\"top:-3em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:3em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\">p</span></span><span style=\"top:-3em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:3em;\"></span><span class=\"accent-body\" style=\"left:-0.1667em;\"><span class=\"mord\">^</span></span></span></span><span class=\"vlist-s\">​</span></span><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.1944em;\"><span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span>","key":"RFplBxltaO"},{"type":"text","value":" should be p.","position":{"start":{"line":22,"column":1},"end":{"line":22,"column":1}},"key":"StWYAit19Z"}],"key":"VcXECybAe5"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":24,"column":1},"end":{"line":24,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"emphasis","position":{"start":{"line":24,"column":1},"end":{"line":24,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"Spread","position":{"start":{"line":24,"column":1},"end":{"line":24,"column":1}},"key":"ADTufyixdi"}],"key":"Z4APoJkJ7j"},{"type":"text","value":": For samples of 100, we would expect sample proportions of females not to stray too far from the population proportion 0.6. Sample proportions lower than 0.5 or higher than 0.7 would be rather surprising. On the other hand, if we were only taking samples of size 10, we would not be at all surprised by a sample proportion of females even as low as 4/10 = 0.4, or as high as 8/10 = 0.8. Thus, sample size plays a role in the spread of the distribution of sample proportion: there should be less spread for larger samples, more spread for smaller samples.","position":{"start":{"line":24,"column":1},"end":{"line":24,"column":1}},"key":"BGl3la7Ygs"}],"key":"PxqHM62UMj"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":26,"column":1},"end":{"line":26,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"emphasis","position":{"start":{"line":26,"column":1},"end":{"line":26,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"Shape","position":{"start":{"line":26,"column":1},"end":{"line":26,"column":1}},"key":"APHQGbRMGs"}],"key":"GItuZf7Z1Y"},{"type":"text","value":": Sample proportions closest to 0.6 would be most common, and sample proportions far from 0.6 in either direction would be progressively less likely. In other words, the shape of the distribution of sample proportion should bulge in the middle and taper at the ends: it should be somewhat ","position":{"start":{"line":26,"column":1},"end":{"line":26,"column":1}},"key":"HfLPHfDu1P"},{"type":"emphasis","position":{"start":{"line":26,"column":1},"end":{"line":26,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"normal.","position":{"start":{"line":26,"column":1},"end":{"line":26,"column":1}},"key":"VOto7tHi3X"}],"key":"LPkwRRjpCB"}],"key":"QzGhiUR8Um"}],"class":"tip","key":"TO5pu4E7mv"},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"position":{"start":{"line":29,"column":1},"end":{"line":29,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"Comment","position":{"start":{"line":29,"column":1},"end":{"line":29,"column":1}},"key":"Zi1jPHf7tA"}],"identifier":"comment","label":"Comment","html_id":"comment","implicit":true,"key":"vv6OpCzdJx"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":31,"column":1},"end":{"line":31,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"The ","position":{"start":{"line":31,"column":1},"end":{"line":31,"column":1}},"key":"uT0FBoHh68"},{"type":"emphasis","position":{"start":{"line":31,"column":1},"end":{"line":31,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"distribution","position":{"start":{"line":31,"column":1},"end":{"line":31,"column":1}},"key":"NbNj3usmVO"}],"key":"gF7MxjNWP9"},{"type":"text","value":" of the values of the sample proportions (","position":{"start":{"line":31,"column":1},"end":{"line":31,"column":1}},"key":"v0dMEFxG0b"},{"type":"inlineMath","value":"\\hat{p}","position":{"start":{"line":31,"column":1},"end":{"line":31,"column":1}},"html":"<span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mover accent=\"true\"><mi>p</mi>