6875a14efdf8d0.mjs","model":{"question":"Suppose p = 0.6 and we repeatedly take random samples of size 100, recording the sample proportion each time. Around what value will the sample proportions center?","choices":[{"correct":true,"text":"0.6","feedback":"Correct! The sample proportions average out to the population proportion, 0.6."},{"correct":false,"text":"0.5","feedback":"0.5 has no special role here; the center follows the population proportion."},{"correct":false,"text":"There is no predictable center","feedback":"The sample proportions cluster around a predictable center: the parameter p."}],"multi":false,"hint":"The mean of the sampling distribution of p-hat is p.","explanation":""},"key":"W3DCZL4ZZ8"},{"type":"anywidget","esm":"/textbook/build/quiz-widget-e9689e083a3d48b3e46875a14efdf8d0.mjs","model":{"question":"Two students each draw many random samples: one uses samples of size 25, the other of size 2,500. How will their sampling distributions of p-hat differ?","choices":[{"correct":true,"text":"Both center at p, but the size-2,500 samples have much less spread","feedback":"Correct! Both are centered at p, but the larger samples produce sample proportions that cluster much more tightly around p—less spread."},{"correct":false,"text":"The size-2,500 samples center closer to p, with the same spread","feedback":"The centers are the same (p); it's the spread that differs."},{"correct":false,"text":"The size-2,500 samples have more spread","feedback":"Larger samples give LESS variability, not more."}],"multi":false,"hint":"Larger samples approximate the population better.","explanation":""},"key":"egRzoJbyHT"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":62,"column":1},"end":{"line":62,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"At this point, we have a good sense of what happens as we take random samples from a population. Our simulation suggests that our initial intuition about the shape and center of the sampling distribution is correct. If the population has a proportion of p, then random samples of the same size drawn from the population will have sample proportions close to p. More specifically, the distribution of sample proportions will have a mean of p. We also observed that for this situation, the sample proportions are approximately normal. We will see later that this is not always the case. But if sample proportions are normally distributed, then the distribution is centered at p. Now we want to think more about the variability we expect to see in the sample proportions. Our intuition tells us that larger samples will better approximate the population, so we might expect less variability in large samples. The next video uses simulations to investigate this idea. After that, we will tie these ideas to more formal theory.","position":{"start":{"line":62,"column":1},"end":{"line":62,"column":1}},"key":"d4uQfwH7vn"}],"key":"yoM8EnRZHk"},{"type":"admonition","kind":"note","children":[{"type":"admonitionTitle","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Video","position":{"start":{"line":64,"column":1},"end":{"line":64,"column":1}},"key":"izrcRSj0j3"}],"key":"uMkYDlJX5n"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":66,"column":1},"end":{"line":66,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"link","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=tUvXeJ3A3_s","position":{"start":{"line":66,"column":1},"end":{"line":66,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"Behavior of Sample Proportion 2","position":{"start":{"line":66,"column":1},"end":{"line":66,"column":1}},"key":"iW15qtRTzq"}],"urlSource":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=tUvXeJ3A3_s","key":"ny5u1AQUZR"}],"key":"DKmLggijQB"}],"key":"FU6Vgnrsic"}],"key":"u8Xkads3jb"}],"key":"QBwRNQGmxi"},"references":{"cite":{"order":[],"data":{}}},"footer":{"navigation":{"prev":{"title":"Parameters vs. Statistics","url":"/pages/m12-parameters-vs-statistics","group":"Section 9: Sampling Distributions"},"next":{"title":"The Sampling Distribution of the Sample Proportion","url":"/pages/m12-behavior-of-sample-proportion-2-of-3","group":"Section 9: Sampling Distributions"}}},"domain":"http://localhost:3000"}